<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A software house based in the UAE built and ran systems for banks, insurers and government entities across the Gulf. Its engineers had embraced AI: coding assistants in their editors, AI-native editors with their own extension marketplaces, hosted AI development platforms for experiments, and personal API keys for several model providers. Developers had local admin rights, long-lived cloud credentials and, in some cases, access to client environments from their laptops.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> October 2026 made the risk concrete. On 29 October, tampered versions of the Claude Code extension were listed in Cursor's extension marketplace for a few hours; Microsoft's analysis describes a loader that tried to add an attacker SSH key and open a reverse tunnel on developer machines<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Days earlier, a Wiz Research report described a month of breaches at AI developer platforms, with stolen AI credentials feeding LLMjacking, the resale of model access<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. For a firm whose clients sit under the UAE's tightening expectations on cyber resilience and supply-chain security<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, "a developer's editor was compromised" could not be allowed to mean "a client was compromised".</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Only approved extensions and tools</strong> on developer machines, updated deliberately rather than automatically.</li>
<li><strong>No long-lived secrets on laptops</strong>: nothing on a developer machine that can reach production or a client on its own.</li>
<li><strong>One governed route to AI models</strong>, with per-team keys, limits and logs.</li>
<li><strong>Only verified builds</strong> reach client environments.</li>
<li><strong>Productivity kept</strong>: engineers still use AI assistants every day.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Developer machine</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Managed endpoint<em>no standing admin, EDR</em></span><span>Approved extensions<em>from a private mirror</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>AI access</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">AI gateway<em>per-team keys, limits, logs</em></span><span>Approved models<em>by data classification</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Identity and secrets</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Short-lived credentials<em>issued on demand</em></span><span>Secrets manager<em>no keys in repos or env files</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Build</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>CI runners<em>isolated, ephemeral</em></span><span class="hl">Signed artefacts<em>provenance recorded</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Client environments</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Admission policy<em>signed images only</em></span><span>Separate per client<em>no shared credentials</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Each layer assumes the one above it might be compromised.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Code</b><span>An engineer writes code with an AI assistant on a managed machine</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Ask</b><span>Assistant calls go through the AI gateway with the team's key, not a personal one</span></li>
<li><b>Commit</b><span>Code is pushed; secrets scanning blocks any credential in the change</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Build</b><span>Ephemeral CI builds, signs the artefact and records its provenance</span></li>
<li><b>Admit</b><span>The client environment admits only artefacts with a valid signature</span></li>
<li><b>Operate</b><span>Production access is granted just in time, never from standing laptop credentials</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>A compromised laptop can affect code, which review and signing catch; it cannot reach a client directly.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: controlling extensions without killing productivity</h2>
<p>Extensions are the riskiest software on a developer's machine: they run with the user's rights, auto-update silently and can come from marketplaces the company does not control. The Cursor incident showed that even a well-known extension name is not a guarantee, and that auto-update can push a tampered build to thousands of machines in hours<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Banning extensions would have failed; engineers would have worked around it. Instead the team built a private mirror. Approved extensions are pulled, scanned and pinned to a version; editors are configured to install only from the mirror; and updates are promoted after a short delay and a check of the publisher's security notices. Requests for new extensions go through a lightweight review with a target turnaround, so the approved list grows with real needs.</p>
<p>The second control was removing what a compromised extension could steal. Long-lived cloud keys, client VPN profiles and personal model API keys were taken off laptops. Developers get short-lived credentials for the task at hand, and AI calls use the gateway, so a stolen key from a laptop or a third-party platform is limited by the gateway's quotas and visible in its logs. See <a href="/insights/ai-coding-policy-engineering-teams/">AI coding policy for engineering teams</a> and <a href="/insights/terraform-secrets-out-of-state/">keeping secrets out of state</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Assume one developer tool will be compromised. Design so that the worst it can do is change code that review and signing will catch, not reach a client or spend unlimited money.</p></div>

<h2>The last line: only verified builds reach clients</h2>
<p>Even with good endpoint controls, code can be tampered with. The final check sits at the client environment: only container images signed by the firm's CI, with recorded provenance, are admitted. Kubernetes supports this pattern through admission policies that validate image signatures before a pod is allowed to run<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/uae-software-house-ai-developer-toolchain-security/signature-policy.webp" alt="Flowchart: create a policy instance and annotate the namespace to validate signatures, create a pod, run policy evaluation, then admit the pod and pull the image if it passes or refuse admission if it fails" width="560" height="1314" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Signature verification at admission: unsigned or tampered images never start. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2023/06/29/container-image-signature-verification/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Sascha Grunert</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Security</small><ol><li>Incident triage</li><li class="hl">Extension review</li><li>Detection rules</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform engineering</small><ol><li>Private mirror</li><li class="hl">AI gateway and secrets</li><li>Signing and admission</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering leads</small><ol><li>Extension requests</li><li>Team AI budgets</li><li class="hl">Adoption and feedback</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Security set the rules, platform made them easy, engineering leads kept them practical.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Week 1</small><b>Contain</b><ul><li>Check for known indicators</li><li>Rotate exposed keys</li><li>Disable auto-update</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 2 to 4</small><b>Mirror and gateway</b><ul><li>Approved extension list</li><li>AI gateway</li><li>Team keys and limits</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 5 to 8</small><b>Remove secrets</b><ul><li>Short-lived credentials</li><li>Client access just in time</li><li>Secrets scanning</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 9 to 12</small><b>Verify</b><ul><li>Signed builds</li><li>Admission policies</li><li>Compromised-tool drill</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Contain the immediate risk in week one; build the lasting controls over a quarter.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Private extension mirror</td><td>Ban extensions or allow any</td><td>Keeps productivity, removes silent updates</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI access through a gateway</td><td>Personal API keys</td><td>Limits, logs and quick revocation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short-lived credentials</td><td>Long-lived keys on laptops</td><td>Stolen credentials expire before they are useful</td></tr>
<tr><td>Signed builds and admission checks</td><td>Trust the CI pipeline</td><td>Tampered code cannot reach clients unnoticed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Separate access per client</td><td>Shared credentials across clients</td><td>One incident cannot spread between clients</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: give engineers a fast path to request tools. The mirror only works if it says yes quickly to reasonable requests. Differently: put the AI gateway in place before AI tools spread, not after. Personal keys scattered across laptops and platforms were the slowest thing to clean up. Related: the <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-01/">1 November tech news</a>, <a href="/insights/binance-hack-custody-architecture-lessons/">the Binance hack: architecture lessons</a>, <a href="/case-studies/us-utility-management-plane-hardening/">hardening a utility's management plane</a> and the <a href="/uae/">UAE page</a>.</p>
<p>If your teams use AI coding tools and serve regulated clients, see my <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
